## Option 1 — inject a signal the test can await (most common fix)

Give the service a `TaskCompletionSource` (or a `Task` it hands out) that is completed when the background loop actually finishes:

```csharp
public sealed class OrderWorker : BackgroundService
{
    private readonly TaskCompletionSource _stopped =
        new(TaskCreationOptions.RunContinuationsAsynchronously);

    // Test-facing completion signal: resolves when the background work is done.
    public Task Completion => _stopped.Task;

    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        try
        {
            while (!stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // ... do the work ...
            }
        }
        finally
        {
            _stopped.TrySetResult();
        }
    }
}
```

In the test, instantiate the service directly, start it, then await the signal:

```csharp
[Fact]
public async Task Worker_ProcessesQueue_WhenStarted()
{
    var worker = new OrderWorker(/* fakes for repository, etc. */);

    await worker.StartAsync(CancellationToken.None);
    // ... enqueue/arrange whatever the background loop consumes ...

    await worker.Completion.WaitAsync(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); // await the real task

    // now assert on the effects
}
```

`WaitAsync(timeout)` (available since .NET 6) is the key detail: it awaits the *real* task but still fails the test instead of hanging forever if the background work never completes.

Note `TaskCreationOptions.RunContinuationsAsynchronously`: without it, completing the TCS from a background thread can synchronously run the test's continuation on that thread, which makes deadlocks and weird timing more likely.

## Option 2 — you're going through the host (`WebApplicationFactory`, `IHost`)

If the test spins up a real host, resolve the worker and await its signal the same way:

```csharp
