```

Don't rely on `IHost.StopAsync` to mean "work finished" — for a typical `BackgroundService` that only meaningfully cancels the loop and leaves actual completion to whatever the loop does on the way out, so the explicit signal is still the thing to await.

## If there's no per-operation signal, use a seam

When the background work's only observable effect is a call to something like a queue consumer or an external service, express "it finished" as a fake you control and await that. With NSubstitute:

```csharp
