consumer
    .When(c => c.Handle(Arg.Any<Order>()))
    .Do(_ => processed.TrySetResult());

// ...start the worker...

await processed.Task.WaitAsync(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
```

Moq equivalent: a `Callback` that sets the same TCS.

## What not to do

- `Thread.Sleep(500)` / `await Task.Delay(500)` before asserting — flaky under load and slow when it passes. This is the exact anti-pattern the checklist calls out.
- `async void` test methods — the runner can't await them, so failures inside are swallowed.
- `.Result` / `.Wait()` on the background task — blocks a thread and risks deadlock.

## Getting the signal in place

The one thing that sometimes needs a production tweak is *having a completion signal at all*. Adding a `Task Completion { get; }` that resolves when the loop exits is a minimal, intent-revealing change and is the cleanest fix — much better than adding an arbitrary delay. If the API genuinely can't expose one, fall back to a fake's callback (the seam approach above) rather than a sleep.

